cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this work, please cite it using the metadata below. Fill in author/affiliation/repo before publication."
title: "Near-Duplicate Leakage in ML Prediction of High-Entropy-Alloy Phase Formation: a reproducible audit"
abstract: >-
  A random train/test split quietly inflates balanced accuracy in machine-learning
  prediction of high-entropy-alloy (HEA) solid-solution formation. Using a public
  dataset (Zenodo 6403257, CC-BY-4.0, n≈1100 alloys), we introduce an
  exclusion-radius sweep that isolates near-duplicate redundancy from data-volume
  effects. For gradient-boosted trees at exclusion radius tau=0.35, removing
  near-duplicates drops balanced accuracy from 0.836 (size-matched control) to 0.685,
  a pure redundancy gap of +0.151. The total accuracy drop from tau=0 to tau=0.35
  (0.198) decomposes into ~24% data volume and ~76% near-duplicate redundancy. All
  numbers regenerate deterministically via reproduce.py. This is an undergraduate-level
  methodological audit, not a new model or theory; it demonstrates a systematic,
  reproducible over-estimation under random splits and does not allege misconduct in
  any specific paper.
